		{
			"id": "114117193749345685",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://floss.social/@webbureaucrat/114117193695109601",
			"author": {
				"name": "webbureaucrat",
				"url": "https://floss.social/@webbureaucrat",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/470/005/055/358/994/original/9d33992f38e0fb47.png"
			},
			"date": "2025-03-06T19:43:06.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> </p><p>I'm struggling to articulate this point so bear with me... I think it's good in the sense that it leads people to think about the underlying philosophy of a product, rather than jumping onto a tool bandwagon and then getting mad it's not all things to all people. Like if I know something about Zach Leatherman and how he thinks about software, I know better whether Eleventy would be a good fit for me and my use case.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "114117201676810822",
			"parentId": "114117193749345685",
			"url": "https://floss.social/@webbureaucrat/114117201577857174",
			"author": {
				"name": "webbureaucrat",
				"url": "https://floss.social/@webbureaucrat",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/470/005/055/358/994/original/9d33992f38e0fb47.png"
			},
			"date": "2025-03-06T19:45:07.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> </p><p>On the other hand, I've seen a *lot* of really good products collapse because being good at managing celebrity status is a very different skill than managing software, and a lot of people just can't do it. (I certainly could never!)</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
